                "content": "# Rh-Catalyzed Carbonylation Cycle\n\n## Part (1): Oxidation States, d-Configurations, and Electron Counts\n\n**Method:** In the ionic (donor-pair) method, assign formal charges to ligands first, then deduce the metal oxidation state from the overall complex charge. Electron count = (d-electrons on metal) + (electrons donated by ligands).\n\n| Complex | Formula | Oxidation State | d\u207f | Electron Count |\n|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| **A** | $[\\mathrm{RhI_2(CO)_2}]^-$ | +1 | d\u2078 | 16 e\u207b |\n| **B** | $[\\mathrm{RhI_3(CH_3)(CO)_2}]^-$ | +3 | d\u2076 | 18 e\u207b |\n| **C** | $[\\mathrm{RhI_3(C(O)CH_3)(CO)}]^-$ | +3 | d\u2076 | 16 e\u207b |\n| **D** | $[\\mathrm{RhI_3(C(O)CH_3)(CO)_2}]^-$ | +3 | d\u2076 | 18 e\u207b |\n\n**Worked examples:**\n\n**A:** $\\mathrm{OS} + 2(-1) + 2(0) = -1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\mathrm{OS} = +1$\n\n$$d\\text{-electrons} = 9 - 1 = 8$$\n$$\\text{Total} = \\underbrace{8}_{\\text{Rh}^{1+}} + \\underbrace{2\\times2}_{\\text{2 I}^-} + \\underbrace{2\\times2}_{\\text{2 CO}} = 16$$\n\n**B:** $\\mathrm{OS} + 3(-1) + 1(-1) + 2(0) = -1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\mathrm{OS} = +3$\n\n$$d\\text{-electrons} = 9 - 3 = 6$$\n$$\\text{Total} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\text{Rh}^{3+}} + \\underbrace{3\\times2}_{\\text{3 I}^-} + \\underbrace{1\\times2}_{\\text{CH}_3^-} + \\underbrace{2\\times2}_{\\text{2 CO}} = 18$$\n\n**C:** $\\mathrm{OS} + 3(-1) + 1(-1) + 1(0) = -1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\mathrm{OS} = +3$\n\n$$\\text{Total} = 6 + 6 + 2 + 2 = 16$$\n\n**D:** $\\mathrm{OS} + 3(-1) + 1(-1) + 2(0) = -1 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\mathrm{OS} = +3$\n\n$$\\text{Total} = 6 + 6 + 2 + 4 = 18$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (2): Step Classification\n\n| Step | Transformation | \u0394 Oxidation State | \u0394 Electron Count |\n|:---:|:---|:---:|:---:|\n| A \u2192 B | **Oxidative addition** (of CH\u2083I) | +1 \u2192 +3 (increases by 2) | 16 \u2192 18 (increases by 2) |\n| B \u2192 C | **Migratory insertion** (CO into Rh\u2013CH\u2083) | +3 \u2192 +3 (no change) | 18 \u2192 16 (decreases by 2) |\n| C \u2192 D | **Ligand association** (CO binding) | +3 \u2192 +3 (no change) | 16 \u2192 18 (increases by 2) |\n| D \u2192 A | **Reductive elimination** (of CH\u2083COI) | +3 \u2192 +1 (decreases by 2) | 18 \u2192 16 (decreases by 2) |\n\n---\n\n## Part (3): Net Reaction\n\nSumming all four steps (intermediates cancel):\n\n$$\\boxed{\\mathrm{CH_3I + CO \\longrightarrow CH_3COI}}$$\n\n**Why CO is both a ligand and a stoichiometric reactant:**\n\nCO plays a **dual role**. Throughout the cycle it acts as a *ligand*: it coordinates to Rh in every intermediate (A, B, C, D), modulating electron count and enabling the elementary steps. However, one CO molecule is *consumed stoichiometrically* because it is the carbon source that becomes the carbonyl carbon in the product acetyl iodide. Specifically, the CO that migrates-inserts into the Rh\u2013CH\u2083 bond (step B \u2192 C) is incorporated into the C(O)CH\u2083 acyl group, and that acyl group is ultimately eliminated as CH\u2083COI. Thus, each turnover consumes one CO molecule from solution to replenish the ligand pool, making CO a stoichiometric reactant in the net equation.\n\n---\n\n## Part (4): Isotopic Labeling \u2014 Atom Tracking\n\n**Starting material:** A contains one $^{13}\\mathrm{CO}$ and one $^{12}\\mathrm{CO}$.\n\n### Step-by-step tracking\n\n**A \u2192 B (oxidative addition):** No CO is lost or gained. B still has one $^{13}\\mathrm{CO}$ and one $^{12}\\mathrm{CO}$.\n\n**B \u2192 C (migratory insertion):** One of the two coordinated CO ligands inserts into the Rh\u2013CH\u2083 bond with equal probability (given assumption (i)).\n\n| Outcome | Probability | Acyl ligand | Remaining coordinated CO |\n|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| $^{13}\\mathrm{CO}$ inserts | 1/2 | $^{13}\\mathrm{C(O)CH_3}$ | $^{12}\\mathrm{CO}$ |\n| $^{12}\\mathrm{CO}$ inserts | 1/2 | $^{12}\\mathrm{C(O)CH_3}$ | $^{13}\\mathrm{CO}$ |\n\n**C \u2192 D (association of external $^{12}\\mathrm{CO}$):**\n\n| Outcome | Acyl | Coordinated COs in D |\n|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| (prob 1/2) | $^{13}\\mathrm{C(O)CH_3}$ | $^{12}\\mathrm{CO}$ (original) + $^{12}\\mathrm{CO}$ (new) |\n| (prob 1/2) | $^{12}\\mathrm{C(O)CH_3}$ | $^{13}\\mathrm{CO}$ (original) + $^{12}\\mathrm{CO}$ (new) |\n\n**D \u2192 A (reductive elimination of CH\u2083COI):** The acyl ligand and one I\u207b are eliminated as the product. The two remaining ligands (three I\u207b minus one, plus the remaining COs) regenerate A.\n\n| Outcome | Product | Regenerated A contains |\n|:---:|:---:|:---:|\n| (prob 1/2) | $^{13}\\mathrm{CH_3COI}$ | two $^{12}\\mathrm{CO}$ |\n| (prob 1/2) | $^{12}\\mathrm{CH_3COI}$ | one $^{13}\\mathrm{CO}$ + one $^{12}\\mathrm{CO}$ |\n\n### Answers\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{Fraction of acetyl iodide with }^{13}\\text{C at carbonyl} = \\frac{1}{2}}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{Fraction of regenerated A retaining the original }^{13}\\text{CO} = \\frac{1}{2}}$$\n\n**Justification:** The $^{13}\\mathrm{C}$ label ends up in the product only if the $^{13}\\mathrm{CO}$ was the ligand that underwent migratory insertion (probability 1/2). Conversely, the $^{13}\\mathrm{CO}$ survives in the regenerated catalyst only if it was *not* the one that inserted (probability 1/2). These two events are mutually exclusive and exhaustive.\n\n---\n\n## Part (5): Rate Law with CO Inhibition\n\n**Given:**\n$$A + \\mathrm{CO} \\rightleftharpoons A_{\\mathrm{CO}}, \\qquad K_{\\mathrm{CO}} = \\frac{[A_{\\mathrm{CO}}]}{[A][\\mathrm{CO}]}$$\n\n$$v = k[A][\\mathrm{CH_3I}], \\qquad [\\mathrm{Rh}]_{\\mathrm{T}} = [A] + [A_{\\mathrm{CO}}]$$\n\n**Derivation:**\n\nFrom the equilibrium expression:\n$$[A_{\\mathrm{CO}}] = K_{\\mathrm{CO}}\\,[A]\\,[\\mathrm{CO}]$$\n\nSubstitute into the mass balance:\n$$[\\mathrm{Rh}]_{\\mathrm{T}} = [A] + K_{\\mathrm{CO}}[A][\\mathrm{CO}] = [A]\\left(1 + K_{\\mathrm{CO}}[\\mathrm{CO}]\\right)$$\n\nSolve for [A]:\n$$[A] = \\frac{[\\mathrm{Rh}]_{\\mathrm{T}}}{1 + K_{\\mathrm{CO}}[\\mathrm{CO}]}$$\n\nSubstitute into the rate expression:\n\n$$\\boxed{v = \\frac{k\\,[\\mathrm{Rh}]_{\\mathrm{T}}\\,[\\mathrm{CH_3I}]}{1 + K_{\\mathrm{CO}}[\\mathrm{CO}]}}$$\n\n**Limiting CO orders:**\n\n- **Low [CO]** ($K_{\\mathrm{CO}}[\\mathrm{CO}] \\ll 1$): the denominator \u2192 1, so\n$$v \\approx k[\\mathrm{Rh}]_{\\mathrm{T}}[\\mathrm{CH_3I}] \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\text{order in CO} = 0$$\n\n- **High [CO]** ($K_{\\mathrm{CO}}[\\mathrm{CO}] \\gg 1$): the denominator \u2192 $K_{\\mathrm{CO}}[\\mathrm{CO}]$, so\n$$v \\approx \\frac{k[\\mathrm{Rh}]_{\\mathrm{T}}[\\mathrm{CH_3I}]}{K_{\\mathrm{CO}}[\\mathrm{CO}]} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\text{order in CO} = -1$$\n\nAt high CO, the catalyst is sequestered as the inactive 18-electron $A_{\\mathrm{CO}}$, and the rate is *inhibited* by CO.\n\n---\n\n## Part (6): Numerical Rate Reduction\n\nThe rate ratio relative to the $[\\mathrm{CO}]\\to 0$ limit is:\n\n$$\\frac{v([\\mathrm{CO}]=0)}{v([\\mathrm{CO}]=0.100)} = \\frac{1}{1 + K_{\\mathrm{CO}}[\\mathrm{CO}]}^{-1} = 1 + K_{\\mathrm{CO}}[\\mathrm{CO}]$$\n\n$$= 1 + (20.0\\;\\mathrm{L\\,mol^{-1}})(0.100\\;\\mathrm{mol\\,L^{-1}}) = 1 + 2.00 = 3.00$$\n\n$$\\boxed{\\text{The rate is reduced by a factor of } 3.}$$\n\n**Physical interpretation:** At this CO concentration, the equilibrium $A + \\mathrm{CO} \\rightleftharpoons A_{\\mathrm{CO}}$ shifts such that only 1/3 of the total rhodium is in the reactive 16-electron form A; the remaining 2/3 is tied up as the inactive 18-electron $A_{\\mathrm{CO}}$, directly suppressing the oxidative addition rate.",
                "reasoning_content": "Let me work through this problem systematically.\n\n**Part (1): Oxidation states, d^n configurations, and electron counts**\n\nUsing the ionic method:\n- Rh is group 9\n- I\u207b is X-type (anionic, -1 charge, 2e donor)\n- CH\u2083\u207b is X-type (anionic, -1 charge, 2e donor)\n- C(O)CH\u2083\u207b (acyl) is X-type (anionic, -1 charge, 2e donor)\n- CO is L-type (neutral, 0 charge, 2e donor)\n\n**Complex A: [RhI\u2082(CO)\u2082]\u207b**\n\nOxidation state: The complex has charge -1.\n- 2 I\u207b ligands contribute -2\n- 2 CO ligands contribute 0\n- So: OS + (-2) + 0 = -1 \u2192 OS = +1\n\nRh(+) is d\u2078 (group 9, so 9 - 1 = 8)\n\nElectron count:\n- Rh(I): 9 - 1 = 8 d-electrons\n- 2 I\u207b: 2 \u00d7 2 = 4 electrons\n- 2 CO: 2 \u00d7 2 = 4 electrons\n- Total: 8 + 4 + 4 = 16 electrons\n\n**Complex B: [RhI\u2083(CH\u2083)(CO)\u2082]\u207b**\n\nOxidation state: The complex has charge -1.\n- 3 I\u207b ligands contribute -3\n- 1 CH\u2083\u207b contributes -1\n- 2 CO contribute 0\n- So: OS + (-3) + (-1) + 0 = -1 \u2192 OS = +3\n\nRh(III) is d\u2076 (9 - 3 = 6)\n\nElectron count:\n- Rh(III): 9 - 3 = 6 d-electrons\n- 3 I\u207b: 3 \u00d7 2 = 6 electrons\n- 1 CH\u2083\u207b: 1 \u00d7 2 = 2 electrons\n- 2 CO: 2 \u00d7 2 = 4 electrons\n- Total: 6 + 6 + 2 + 4 = 18 electrons\n\n**Complex C: [RhI\u2083(C(O)CH\u2083)(CO)]\u207b**\n\nOxidation state: The complex has charge -1.\n- 3 I\u207b ligands contribute -3\n- 1 acyl ligand contributes -1\n- 1 CO contributes 0\n- So: OS + (-3) + (-1) + 0 = -1 \u2192 OS = +3\n\nRh(III) is d\u2076\n\nElectron count:\n- Rh(III): 6 d-electrons\n- 3 I\u207b: 3 \u00d7 2 = 6 electrons\n- 1 acyl: 1 \u00d7 2 = 2 electrons\n- 1 CO: 1 \u00d7 2 = 2 electrons\n- Total: 6 + 6 + 2 + 2 = 16 electrons\n\n**Complex D: [RhI\u2083(C(O)CH\u2083)(CO)\u2082]\u207b**\n\nOxidation state: The complex has charge -1.\n- 3 I\u207b ligands contribute -3\n- 1 acyl ligand contributes -1\n- 2 CO contribute 0\n- So: OS + (-3) + (-1) + 0 = -1 \u2192 OS = +3\n\nRh(III) is d\u2076\n\nElectron count:\n- Rh(III): 6 d-electrons\n- 3 I\u207b: 3 \u00d7 2 = 6 electrons\n- 1 acyl: 1 \u00d7 2 = 2 electrons\n- 2 CO: 2 \u00d7 2 = 4 electrons\n- Total: 6 + 6 + 2 + 4 = 18 electrons\n\n**Part (2): Classification and changes**\n\nA \u2192 B: Oxidative addition of CH\u2083I\n- Oxidation state: +1 \u2192 +3 (increases by 2)\n- Electron count: 16 \u2192 18 (increases by 2)\n\nB \u2192 C: Migratory insertion (CO inserts into Rh-CH\u2083 bond)\n- Oxidation state: +3 \u2192 +3 (no change)\n- Electron count: 18 \u2192 16 (decreases by 2)\n\nC \u2192 D: Ligand association (CO binds)\n- Oxidation state: +3 \u2192 +3 (no change)\n- Electron count: 16 \u2192 18 (increases by 2)\n\nD \u2192 A: Reductive elimination (CH\u2083COI eliminated)\n- Oxidation state: +3 \u2192 +1 (decreases by 2)\n- Electron count: 18 \u2192 16 (decreases by 2)\n\n**Part (3): Net reaction**\n\nAdding all steps:\nA + CH\u2083I \u2192 B\nB \u2192 C\nC + CO \u2192 D\nD \u2192 A + CH\u2083COI\n\nNet: CH\u2083I + CO \u2192 CH\u2083COI\n\nSo the net reaction is: CH\u2083I + CO \u2192 CH\u2083COI (acetyl iodide)\n\nCO is a ligand in the cycle (it coordinates to Rh in complexes A, B, C, D) and it is a stoichiometric reactant because one CO molecule is incorporated into the product (acetyl iodide). The CO that ends up in the product was originally a coordinated ligand that got inserted into the Rh-CH\u2083 bond. So CO serves dual roles: it's a ligand that facilitates the reaction (by coordinating and then being inserted), and it's consumed stoichiometrically (one CO per turnover ends up in the product).\n\n**Part (4): Isotopic labeling**\n\nStarting catalyst A has one \u00b9\u00b3CO and one \u00b9\u00b2CO.\n\nStep A \u2192 B: Oxidative addition of CH\u2083I. The two CO ligands remain coordinated. B has one \u00b9\u00b3CO and one \u00b9\u00b2CO.\n\nStep B \u2192 C: Migratory insertion. One of the two CO ligands inserts into the Rh-CH\u2083 bond. Since both CO ligands are equivalent and insertion chooses either with equal probability:\n- With probability 1/2: \u00b9\u00b3CO inserts \u2192 acyl ligand is \u00b9\u00b3C(O)CH\u2083, remaining coordinated CO is \u00b9\u00b2CO\n- With probability 1/2: \u00b9\u00b2CO inserts \u2192 acyl ligand is \u00b9\u00b2C(O)CH\u2083, remaining coordinated CO is \u00b9\u00b3CO\n\nStep C \u2192 D: Association of external CO (exclusively \u00b9\u00b2CO).\n- Case 1 (prob 1/2): D has acyl = \u00b9\u00b3C(O)CH\u2083, coordinated COs = \u00b9\u00b2CO (original) + \u00b9\u00b2CO (new)\n- Case 2 (prob 1/2): D has acyl = \u00b9\u00b2C(O)CH\u2083, coordinated COs = \u00b9\u00b3CO (original) + \u00b9\u00b2CO (new)\n\nStep D \u2192 A: Reductive elimination of CH\u2083COI.\n- Case 1 (prob 1/2): Product is \u00b9\u00b3CH\u2083COI (contains \u00b9\u00b3C at carbonyl carbon). Regenerated A has two \u00b9\u00b2CO ligands.\n- Case 2 (prob 1/2): Product is \u00b9\u00b2CH\u2083COI (contains \u00b9\u00b2C at carbonyl carbon). Regenerated A has one \u00b9\u00b3CO and one \u00b9\u00b2CO.\n\nSo:\n- Fraction of acetyl iodide containing \u00b9\u00b3C at carbonyl carbon: 1/2\n- Fraction of regenerated A molecules still containing the original \u00b9\u00b3CO: 1/2\n\n**Part (5): Rate law derivation**\n\nWe have:\n- A + CO \u21cc A_CO, with K_CO = [A_CO]/([A][CO])\n- Rate: v = k[A][CH\u2083I]\n- [Rh]_T = [A] + [A_CO]\n\nFrom the equilibrium: [A_CO] = K_CO [A][CO]\n\nSo: [Rh]_T = [A] + K_CO [A][CO] = [A](1 + K_CO[CO])\n\nTherefore: [A] = [Rh]_T / (1 + K_CO[CO])\n\nRate: v = k[A][CH\u2083I] = k[Rh]_T[CH\u2083I] / (1 + K_CO[CO])\n\nLimiting orders in CO:\n- Low [CO] (K_CO[CO] << 1): v \u2248 k[Rh]_T[CH\u2083I] \u2192 zero order in CO\n- High [CO] (K_CO[CO] >> 1): v \u2248 k[Rh]_T[CH\u2083I] / (K_CO[CO]) \u2192 first order in 1/[CO], i.e., inverse first order in CO (or -1 order in CO)\n\n**Part (6): Rate reduction factor**\n\nAt [CO] \u2192 0: v_0 = k[Rh]_T[CH\u2083I]\n\nAt [CO] = 0.100 M: v = k[Rh]_T[CH\u2083I] / (1 + K_CO[CO]) = k[Rh]_T[CH\u2083I] / (1 + 20.0 \u00d7 0.100) = k[Rh]_T[CH\u2083I] / (1 + 2.00) = k[Rh]_T[CH\u2083I] / 3.00\n\nFactor of reduction: v_0 / v = 3.00\n\nSo the rate is reduced by a factor of 3.\n\nLet me double-check all my work...\n\nFor Part 1, let me verify electron counts:\n\nA: [RhI\u2082(CO)\u2082]\u207b\n- Rh(I), d\u2078\n- Ligand electrons: 2(2) + 2(2) = 8\n- Total: 8 + 8 = 16 \u2713\n\nB: [RhI\u2083(CH\u2083)(CO)\u2082]\u207b\n- Rh(III), d\u2076\n- Ligand electrons: 3(2) + 1(2) + 2(2) = 12\n- Total: 6 + 12 = 18 \u2713\n\nC: [RhI\u2083(C(O)CH\u2083)(CO)]\u207b\n- Rh(III), d\u2076\n- Ligand electrons: 3(2) + 1(2) + 1(2) = 10\n- Total: 6 + 10 = 16 \u2713\n\nD: [RhI\u2083(C(O)CH\u2083)(CO)\u2082]\u207b\n- Rh(III), d\u2076\n- Ligand electrons: 3(2) + 1(2) + 2(2) = 12\n- Total: 6 + 12 = 18 \u2713\n\nEverything checks out. Let me now write a clean, comprehensive answer.\n"
